total sets: 5995   overall max k: 14

READING: if max k grows like S, the ratio in the last column stays roughly constant;
if it grows like log S, the column falls steeply. Either way a bound k <= f(S) is
enough for ExistenceAt8, since f(S) is finite for every difference set.

LIMIT OF THIS TABLE, and it is the important one.
  These are RANDOM sets. Random sampling gives max k = 14 even at spread 4096-32768,
  but the hill-climb in bounded_k_attack_v1 found k = 369 at spread ~14,125 -- 26x
  worse at comparable spread. So the hard sets are RARE and random sampling does not
  reach them. This table is evidence about typical behaviour and says almost nothing
  about the worst case, which is what a theorem needs.

  Reporting the falling last column as 'k grows like log S' would therefore be exactly
  the error this session has made repeatedly: taking the reach of a search for the
  shape of the thing. The growth rate of the WORST case is not measured here.
